```c
#include <locale.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char* get_codepage() {
    const char* current_locale = setlocale(LC_ALL, "");
    if (current_locale == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }
    size_t len = strlen(current_locale);
    char* codepage = malloc(len + 1);
    strcpy(codepage, current_locale);
    codepage[len] = '\0'; // null-terminate the string
    return codepage;
}
```
This function uses the `setlocale` function from the standard C library to get the current locale, and then extracts the codepage from the locale string. The `LC_ALL` category is used to get the complete locale name.

Note that this implementation assumes that the locale name contains the codepage information. On some platforms, such as Windows, the locale name may not contain the codepage explicitly, but instead rely on other mechanisms to determine the codepage. In those cases, this function may return an incomplete or incorrect result.

Also note that this implementation does not handle errors or edge cases well. For example, if `setlocale` returns an error (which it will do if the locale name is invalid), the function will simply return `NULL`. You may want to add additional error handling and validation depending on your specific use case.

