using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

public int ExecuteCommand(string command, string arguments, Action<string> outputCallback)
{
    ProcessStartInfo startInfo = new ProcessStartInfo
    {
        FileName = command,
        Arguments = arguments,
        RedirectStandardOutput = true,
        RedirectStandardError = true,
        UseShellExecute = false,
        CreateNoWindow = true,
        StandardOutputEncoding = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8,
        StandardErrorEncoding = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8
    };

    Process process = new Process { StartInfo = startInfo };
    process.OutputDataReceived += (sender, data) => outputCallback(data.Data);
    process.ErrorDataReceived += (sender, data) => outputCallback(data.Data);

    process.Start();
    process.BeginOutputReadLine();
    process.BeginErrorReadLine();

    process.WaitForExit();

    return process.ExitCode;
}
```
Here's a breakdown of the function:

* It takes three parameters: `command` (the executable to run), `arguments` (the arguments to pass to the command), and `outputCallback` (a callback function that will be called for each line of output or error).
* It creates a `ProcessStartInfo` object to configure the process.
* It sets up two event handlers, `OutputDataReceived` and `ErrorDataReceived`, which will call the `outputCallback` function whenever the process produces output or error.
* It starts the process and begins reading its output and error streams using `BeginOutputReadLine()` and `BeginErrorReadLine()`.
* Finally, it waits for the process to exit using `WaitForExit()` and returns its exit code.

Note that this function assumes that the callback function will handle the output and error data by writing it to some kind of log or console. You'll need to modify the callback function to suit your needs.

