using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

public static string GetAuthorizationCode()
{
    // Start the process to open the URI
    ProcessStartInfo psi = new ProcessStartInfo("explorer", "https://example.com/authorize");
    psi.CreateNoWindow = true;
    psi.UseShellExecute = true;
    Process.Start(psi);

    // Wait for the process to start (important!)
    System.Threading.Thread.Sleep(500); // adjust this value as needed

    // Read the authorization code from the console
    Console.Write("Enter authorization code: ");
    string code = Console.ReadLine();

    return code;
}
```
Here's how the method works:

1. We create a `ProcessStartInfo` object to start the process that opens the given URI using the "explorer" application (which is usually set as the default browser).
2. We set `CreateNoWindow` to `true` so that the window doesn't appear on screen.
3. We set `UseShellExecute` to `true` so that the process is executed using the Windows shell (instead of creating a new process).
4. We start the process using `Process.Start(psi)`.
5. We wait for 500 milliseconds (you can adjust this value as needed, depending on how long it takes for the browser to open and display the authorization code prompt).
6. We read the authorization code from the console using `Console.ReadLine()`.
7. We return the code as a string.

Note that you should replace `"https://example.com/authorize"` with the actual URI that prompts the user for an authorization code.

