Here is a simple Java program that uses the `jarsigner` command-line tool to sign a JAR file:

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class SignCode {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String jarFile = "path/to/your/jarfile.jar";
        String keyStore = "path/to/your/keystore";
        String alias = "your_alias";

        if (args.length > 0) {
            signJar(jarFile, keyStore, alias, args[0]);
        } else {
            signJar(jarFile, keyStore, alias);
        }
    }

    public static void signJar(String jarFile, String keyStore, String alias) {
        try {
            Runtime.getRuntime().exec("jarsigner -keystore " + keyStore + " -signedjar signed_" + jarFile + " " + jarFile + " " + alias);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error signing the JAR file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public static void signJar(String jarFile, String keyStore, String alias, String password) {
        try {
            Runtime.getRuntime().exec("jarsigner -keystore " + keyStore + " -storepass " + password + " -signedjar signed_" + jarFile + " " + jarFile + " " + alias);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error signing the JAR file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

To use this program, you need to have `jarsigner` in your system's PATH. If not, you can specify the full path of `jarsigner` instead of just `"jarsigner"`.

Also note that the alias is used to select the key to use for signing, and the password is used to unlock the keystore if it's protected with a password.

