Note that this function returns an integer indicating the success of the operation. If the response code is 200, it returns 0. If there was an error, it returns a non-zero value (-1 or -2 in this example). You can modify this behavior to suit your needs.

Also, keep in mind that this function assumes that the JSON payload is a string. In a real-world application, you would likely want to use a `JSONObject` or another data structure to represent the payload.

